<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
  <!-- Android 13+ requires this to be granted at runtime before a notification
       can be shown at all. Asked for on first launch, because a fleet app that
       cannot notify you is missing the point of carrying it. -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

  <!-- NO BACKUP. The credential is sealed with a Keystore key that never
       leaves this device, so a restored copy would be bytes nothing can open —
       but "would fail to decrypt" is an argument, and the manifest was leaving
       it at the default, which is yes. Said here, like every other refusal in
       this file: the coordinator address and the signed-in email are the two
       things a backup would carry in the clear, and a fresh install asks for
       both in under a minute. -->
  <application
      android:label="Fleetwright"
      android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
      android:allowBackup="false"
      android:supportsRtl="true"
      android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
      android:theme="@style/Theme.Fleetwright">

    <!-- ERROR REPORTING, AND WHAT IT IS FORBIDDEN TO SEND.
         A DSN is not a secret: it identifies a project and grants only the
         ability to post events to it, which is why it sits in the manifest
         rather than in a secret store.

         The rest of these are refusals, and they matter more. This app holds a
         fleet credential, a coordinator address and the signed-in email:

         - send-default-pii OFF keeps the user's IP and identifiers out.
         - screenshot and view-hierarchy OFF: the session list, the pane and the
           credentials sheet are all on screen, and a screenshot of any of them
           is the thing the whole app is careful about.
         - breadcrumbs for network requests OFF: this app's requests are intents
           to the coordinator, and their URLs can carry ?token=.
         - user-interaction breadcrumbs OFF: they record which control was
           tapped, by resource id, on screens where the ids name people.

         What is left is the crash and the stack, which is what this is for. -->
    <meta-data android:name="io.sentry.dsn"
        android:value="https://a9b18b2c6f1215fcb90fa31b1478f537@o4512012096765952.ingest.us.sentry.io/4512012110790656" />
    <meta-data android:name="io.sentry.send-default-pii" android:value="false" />
    <meta-data android:name="io.sentry.attach-screenshot" android:value="false" />
    <meta-data android:name="io.sentry.attach-view-hierarchy" android:value="false" />
    <meta-data android:name="io.sentry.traces.user-interaction.enable" android:value="false" />
    <meta-data android:name="io.sentry.breadcrumbs.http" android:value="false" />
    <meta-data android:name="io.sentry.breadcrumbs.user-interaction" android:value="false" />
    <!-- Not 1.0. Tracing is off entirely: this app's spans would be its own
         requests to the coordinator, which is the one thing not to record. -->
    <meta-data android:name="io.sentry.traces.sample-rate" android:value="0.0" />

    <!-- SESSION REPLAY, WHICH IS A RECORDING OF THIS APP'S SCREENS AND IS THE
         ONE THING IN THIS BLOCK THAT IS NOT A REFUSAL.

         The keys above refuse a still of the screen; these ask for the
         sequence of them. Worth stating, because somebody reading
         attach-screenshot four lines up will reasonably wonder.

         A REPLAY FRAME IS COMPOSITED, NOT CAPTURED. Every text view and every
         image view is a rectangle before the frame is encoded, so what leaves
         the phone is the shape of the app - which screen, which sheet, in
         which order - and none of the words on it. That order is the part a
         stack trace does not have; a single masked still is not, which is why
         the trade comes out differently for the two.

         THE MASKING IS WRITTEN OUT THOUGH BOTH ARE ALREADY THE SDK'S DEFAULT.
         A default is something a minor version may change and a line is not,
         and these two are the entire reason the feature is defensible in an
         app holding a fleet credential, a coordinator address and a signed-in
         email. Nothing downstream catches them if they go - a frame is not an
         event and passes through no hook.

         SAME FOUR VALUES AS iOS, deliberately: FleetwrightApp.swift carries
         the long version of the argument, and test/sentry-scrub.test.js reads
         both files and fails if they ever stop matching. These keys are also
         literals from Sentry's documentation rather than names inferred from
         the Swift beside them - a meta-data name the SDK does not recognise is
         SILENTLY IGNORED, so a typo here is a replay that never records and
         says nothing about it.

         The canvas screenshot strategy is the stronger guarantee and was not
         taken; docs/error-reporting.md says why, and it is one line. -->
    <meta-data android:name="io.sentry.session-replay.mask-all-text" android:value="true" />
    <meta-data android:name="io.sentry.session-replay.mask-all-images" android:value="true" />
    <!-- Every session that goes wrong, which is the whole point: the previous
         thirty seconds, attached to the error, without anybody having to
         reproduce it. -->
    <meta-data android:name="io.sentry.session-replay.on-error-sample-rate" android:value="1.0" />
    <!-- AND NO AMBIENT RECORDING. The quickstart says 0.1 and that is the one
         number here it does not get: one session in ten would be a recording
         of somebody's fleet made with no incident behind it and nothing
         waiting to read it, which is collection this file has spent every
         other line declining. Same argument that put traces.sample-rate above
         at 0.0 and the Worker's at 0.05 rather than 1.0.

         It costs nothing diagnostic - the line above already covers every
         session that breaks, and a session that did not is not one anybody
         opens. Raising it is this one line, and it is the line to raise while
         testing the feature, since until somebody does a replay only ever
         appears attached to an error. -->
    <meta-data android:name="io.sentry.session-replay.session-sample-rate" android:value="0.0" />

    <activity
        android:name=".MainActivity"
        android:exported="true"
        android:launchMode="singleTask"
        android:label="Fleetwright">
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
      </intent-filter>

      <!--
        COMING BACK FROM A PROVIDER. The coordinator's OAuth callback redirects
        to fleetwright://connected once it has stored the token, so the browser
        hands control back instead of leaving somebody on a page telling them
        to close a tab.

        singleTask so returning resumes the app that started the flow rather
        than stacking a second copy of it on top.

        NOT an App Link: those need a domain association file and a verified
        apex, which is real setup for anybody self-hosting this. A custom
        scheme is unverified — any app may claim it — which is exactly why the
        redirect carries only `provider` and `ok`, and no credential ever
        travels this way.
      -->
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
        <data android:scheme="fleetwright" android:host="connected" />
      </intent-filter>
    </activity>

    <!--
      A BUTTON ON A NOTIFICATION, PRESSED, and it must not open the app.

      A receiver rather than an activity is the whole point: answering a session
      from a lock screen and being dropped into a session list is not answering
      it from a lock screen, it is opening the app with an extra step in front.

      NOT EXPORTED. The only thing that starts it is a PendingIntent this app
      built, and the extras it carries are a session name, a prompt id and the
      digit to type — which together are an answer. An exported receiver would
      let any app on the phone send one.
    -->
    <receiver
        android:name=".AnswerReceiver"
        android:exported="false" />

    <!--
      Registered so FCM can hand over a rotated token. Without it the app finds
      out about a new token only on the next launch, and until then the
      coordinator is sending to a token nothing will deliver.
    -->
    <service
        android:name=".Messaging"
        android:exported="false">
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="com.google.firebase.MESSAGING_EVENT" />
      </intent-filter>
    </service>
  </application>
</manifest>
